From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:02:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: s5l8960x: The touchscreen on every A7 iPad

The touchscreen was described for the J72 and, from #207, the J85 alone.
Apple's device trees describe the same controller on the same pins
within each model (iOS 12.5.7 and 12.5.8):

  iPad Air (J71, J72, J73)            /arm-io/spi2/multi-touch,
                                      multi-touch,j72: IRQ/attention
                                      GPIO 111, reset GPIO 15
  iPad mini 2 and 3 (J85-J87,         /arm-io/spi1/multi-touch,
  J85m-J87m)                          multi-touch,j85: IRQ/attention
                                      GPIO 15, reset GPIO 23

with display sync on GPIO 119 and the D2089's analog supply and LDO on
all of them. Move each board file's touchscreen into its model's shared
file, so the J72 and J85 nodes serve the whole model. The firmware stays
the model's: J71, J72 and J73 ship the same J72.mtprops, J85, J86 and
J87 the same J85.mtprops, and the iPad mini 3 ships none of its own; its
tree names the j85 controller.

The iPhone 5s is left out: its touch path is a different one. Its
controller (multi-touch,n51, IRQ GPIO 22) takes its analog supply from
the Chestnut display PMU (/arm-io/i2c0/display-pmu) and its LDO from a
D2045 PMIC (pmu,d2045, the kernel's amber PMIC), which has no touch
regulator, and N51.mtprops needs its own firmware conversion.

Assisted-by: LLM
